---
name: beam-message-rail-federation
description: Build a message rail — a microservice implementing IFederatedMessageRail that delivers a campaign's push, email, or in-game messages and reports a per-recipient funnel result.
---

# Message Rail Federation

> General microservice workflow: `beam-manage-service`. Bundling: `beam-bundles`. Campaign side: `beam-campaign-api`.

## High-Level Architecture

```
Campaign Send node ──▶ Message Rail runtime: stage → coalesce → page by maxBatchSize
                                       │
                                       ▼  SendMessageBatch(recipients, payload)
                         customer rail microservice ──▶ provider (APNs / ESP / mailbox)
                                       │
                                       ▼  MessageRailSendResponse (per-recipient sent / error)
                       Message Rail runtime: reconcile → retry retriable / stop terminal
                                       │
                                       ▼
                       funnel stages (Sent, Delivered, Retry…) ──▶ campaign funnel
Player opens/clicks ──(beam_outreach / openTrackingUrl)──▶ analytics ──▶ Opened / Clicked

Campaign publish ──▶ CheckMessageRailConfig ──▶ OK / MISSING / INVALID / UNKNOWN
```

## Code Mapping

| Part | File |
|---|---|
| Interface, DTOs, `MessageRailContract` constants | BeamableProduct `cli/beamable.common/Runtime/Federation/FederatedMessageRail.cs` |
| Route mounting (skips `[Callable]` federation methods) | BeamableProduct `microservice/beamable.tooling.common/Microservice/ServiceMethodHelper.cs` |
| Federation discovery / registration | BeamableProduct `microservice/beamable.tooling.common/Microservice/FederatedComponentGenerator.cs` |
| Federation id validation | BeamableProduct `templates/MicroserviceSourceGen/MicroserviceSourceGen/Analyzers/FederationAnalyzer.cs` |
| Client mail attribution (`beam_outreach`) | BeamableProduct `cli/beamable.common/Runtime/Api/Mail/AbsMailApi.cs` |
| Platform → rail federation calls (send, config check) | BeamableAPI `BeamableShared/Federation/MessageRail/MessageRailFederation.cs` |
| Staging / reconcile | BeamableAPI `BeamableShared/Services/MessageRail/Core/RailCore.cs` |
| Dispatch + rate limit | BeamableAPI `BeamableShared/Services/MessageRail/Runtime/RailDispatch.cs` |
| Delivery consumer, retries, DLQ | BeamableAPI `BeamableShared/Services/MessageRail/Runtime/RailDelivery.cs` |
| Rail discovery | BeamableAPI `BeamableShared/Services/MessageRail/Runtime/RailFederationDiscovery.cs` |
| Open-tracking pixel token | BeamableAPI `BeamableShared/Services/MessageRail/Analytics/RailOpenTracking.cs` |
| Runtime host | BeamableAPI `BeamableMessageRailRuntime/Program.cs` |
| Reference rails (email, push, in-game) | agentic-portal `bundles/features/message-rail/microservices/{Email,Push,InGame}MessageRailService/` |

## Business Goals Constraints

- A rail is the last mile of campaign outreach. Beamable ships no rail; every rail is a customer microservice, so studios choose their own provider and keep their own credentials.
- The rail owns only **provider I/O** and **truthful per-recipient reporting**. Batching, retries, back-off, dedup and funnel counting are the platform's, driven by that report.
- The federation id names the **channel** (`push`, `email`, `sms`), not the vendor: producers target the id, so swapping providers must not change it.
- Publish-time preflight exists so a campaign cannot launch onto a rail missing credentials.
- Attribution (`outreachId`, `openTrackingUrl`) is how Opened/Clicked join back to a recipient and campaign node.

## Performance Constraints

- **Dispatch**: per-CID token bucket 500/s (burst 1000); priority/default lanes drain ~4:1 (8 vs 2 concurrent work items), default never starved.
- **Send call timeout**: 30 s per federation call. Size `maxBatchSize` so one provider page fits comfortably.
- **Config check timeout**: 5 s — it must read local configuration only, no provider round-trip.
- **Retries**: up to 5 delivery attempts, then DLQ (7-day retention). Retriable failures stay pending; terminal stop.

## Usage Guide

### 1. Create the service

```
beam_exec("project new service <Name>MessageRailService -q")
```
{{ if commands["project new service"] }}
#### `beam project new service` options
| Option | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
{{ for opt in commands["project new service"].options }}`{{ opt.name }}` | {{ opt.type }} | {{ opt.description }}
{{ end }}{{ end }}

Storage only if needed (push: device tokens; email/in-game keep consent in stats):

```
beam_exec("project new storage <Name>RailStorage -q")
beam_exec("project deps add <Name>MessageRailService <Name>RailStorage")
```

### 2. Federation id

```csharp
[FederationId(Id)]
public class SmsRailId : IFederationId
{
    public const string Id = "sms";
}
```

Starts with a letter; letters, digits, `_` only. It is the route segment producers target, so changing it breaks them.

### 3. Implement all five members

```csharp
[Microservice("SmsMessageRailService")]
public class SmsMessageRailService : Microservice, IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>
{
    async Promise<MessageRailSendResponse> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.SendMessage(
        MessageRailRecipient recipient, MessageRailPayload payload)
        => await DeliverBatch(new List<MessageRailRecipient> { recipient }, payload);

    async Promise<MessageRailSendResponse> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.SendMessageBatch(
        List<MessageRailRecipient> recipients, MessageRailPayload payload)
        => await DeliverBatch(recipients, payload);

    async Promise<MessageRailRegistrationResponse> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.RegisterUserWithMessageRail(
        string playerId, Dictionary<string, string> registrationData) { /* opt in */ }

    async Promise<MessageRailRegistrationResponse> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.UnregisterUserWithMessageRail(
        string playerId) { /* opt out */ }

    async Promise<MessageRailConfigStatus> IFederatedMessageRail<SmsRailId>.CheckMessageRailConfig()
        { /* step 6 */ }
}
```

- Explicit interface implementations.
- **No `[Callable]` on federation methods** — route mounting skips them; the rail registers but is unreachable.
- The platform only calls `SendMessageBatch`; `SendMessage` delegates to the same `DeliverBatch`.
- Registration is automatic via reflection; no CLI command (`beam federation add` throws).

`Program.cs` is stock: `BeamServer.Create().IncludeRoutes<SmsMessageRailService>(routePrefix: "").RunForever()`. The generated `.csproj` needs nothing extra; set `<BeamServiceGroup>` by hand if the group name contains a hyphen.

### 4. Per-recipient result

```csharp
var response = new MessageRailSendResponse();
response.@params["provider"] = "twilio";
response.@params["delivery"] = "durable";   // only if no delivery receipt will ever follow
response.maxBatchSize = 500;                // provider cap; 0 = unlimited

response.sentPlayers.Add(gamerTag.ToString());
response.sentPayloadFed[gamerTag.ToString()] = payload.extraDataFed;

response.errorPlayersWithStatus.Add(new MessageRailErrorPlayerStatus
{
    playerId = gamerTag.ToString(), status = "network", message = "provider timed out", retriable = true,
});
```

- Every input player exactly once across `sentPlayers` / `errorPlayersWithStatus`; a missing player is silently retried.
- Classify retriable vs terminal where the failure is detected.
- Set `params` once at construction, not per return path; without `delivery = "durable"` the Delivered stage stays zero.
- Overflow past `maxBatchSize` → `MessageRailContract.OverCapacityStatus`, `retriable = true`. The platform caches the cap and resends in right-sized pages.
- Nothing throws out of `DeliverBatch`: an escaped exception discards outcomes already earned and redelivers the whole batch.
- Order: payload + credential checks (batch-wide) → per-recipient gates (consent, address) → one provider call per page.

| status | retriable | means |
|---|---|---|
| `network` | true | 5xx, timeout, transport, failed credential read |
| `over-capacity` | true | past `maxBatchSize` |
| `notConfigured` | true if store unreadable; false if credentials rejected | cannot deliver |
| `invalid` | false | bad payload, no address, 4xx, dead token |
| `optedOut` | false | no consent on this rail |
| `unknown` | false | nothing to deliver to |

### 5. Attribution and tracking pixel

Both arrive per recipient on `MessageRailRecipient`.

- **`outreachId`** — embed in the rendered message/deep link under `MessageRailContract.OutreachKey` (`beam_outreach`), with the payload's `analyticsTrackRef` as `trackId`. Without them Opened/Clicked cannot attribute.
- **`openTrackingUrl`** — render verbatim as a 1x1 image; render nothing when empty (tracking off). Never build it yourself: it carries a platform-signed token with the gamer tag.
- Turn the provider's own open/click tracking **off** (second pixel, rewritten CTA href).
- Push ignores the pixel; the handset echoes `beam_outreach`.

```csharp
if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(pixelUrl))
{
    var pixel = $"<img src=\"{HtmlEscape(pixelUrl)}\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\">";
    var closing = body.LastIndexOf("</body>", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
    body = closing >= 0 ? body.Insert(closing, pixel) : body + pixel;
}
```

### 6. Publish preflight

```csharp
return new MessageRailConfigStatus
{
    configured = false,
    missingKeys = new List<string> { "twilio_sms/account_sid", "twilio_sms/auth_token" },
    message = "Set the Twilio credentials in Realm Secrets.",
};
```

| State | Fields | Publish |
|---|---|---|
| OK | `configured = true` | proceeds |
| MISSING | `configured = false`, `missingKeys` | blocked |
| INVALID | `configured = false`, `invalidReason` | blocked |
| UNKNOWN | `readable = false` | never blocked (outage, not misconfiguration) |

List **every** missing key, fully qualified. `readable` defaults to `true`.

### 7. Credentials and consent

Realm Secrets via the raw path (the generated client escapes `/` in the key → 400). Swallow only 404 so MISSING stays distinct from UNKNOWN:

```csharp
private async Task<string> TryGetRealmSecret(string key)
{
    try
    {
        return await Requester.Request<string>(
            Method.GET,
            $"/api/customers/{Context.Cid}/realms/{Context.Pid}/secrets/values/{key}",
            parser: json =>
            {
                using var doc = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
                return doc.RootElement.TryGetProperty("value", out var v) &&
                       v.ValueKind == JsonValueKind.String ? v.GetString() : null;
            });
    }
    catch (RequesterException ex) when (ex.Status == 404)
    {
        return null;
    }
}
```

Consent in a public game stat (searchable roster); absent = blocked:

```csharp
await Services.Stats.SetStat(StatsDomainType.Game, StatsAccessType.Public,
    playerId, "sms_delivery_enabled", enabled ? "true" : "false");
```

Refusing an opt-in is `success = false` on a 200, not an exception.

### 8. Run and verify

Add the service (and storage) to the bundle manifest `components` (`beam-bundles`), then:

```
beam_exec("project run --ids <Name>MessageRailService")
```

The OpenAPI document must contain `<federationId>/SendMessage`, `SendMessageBatch`, `RegisterUserWithMessageRail`, `UnregisterUserWithMessageRail`, `CheckMessageRailConfig`. Sends that stay staged with the service never called mean the platform's message-rail runtime is not running.
